Three secrets are hardcoded in centroid-is/CentroidX docker-compose.yml, so the
installer cannot set them. Apply this in CentroidX (separate PR) for the
VNC/database prompts to take effect; until then the prompts are collected and
written to /home/centroid/.env but nothing reads them.

  docker-compose.yml:38   echo 'centroid:foo' | chpasswd
                       -> echo "centroid:${VNC_PASSWORD:?}" | chpasswd

  docker-compose.yml:146  SECRET_FILE_TEST_PASSWORD: TODOSetThisStrongPassword
                       -> SECRET_FILE_TEST_PASSWORD: ${FLUTTER_KEYRING_PASSWORD:?}

  docker-compose.yml:252  CENTROID_PGPASSWORD: FooBarHelloWorld     (profiler)
  docker-compose.yml:390  CENTROID_PGPASSWORD: FooBarHelloWorld     (backend)
                       -> CENTROID_PGPASSWORD: ${DB_PASSWORD:?}

  docker-compose.yml:407  - POSTGRES_PASSWORD=FooBarHelloWorld      (timescaledb)
                       -> - POSTGRES_PASSWORD=${DB_PASSWORD:?}

The `:?` form makes compose refuse to start rather than silently fall back to a
default, which is the behaviour that let RENDER_GID=992 ship to every station.

Note for existing stations: changing POSTGRES_PASSWORD does not change the
password of an already-initialised database. timescaledb only reads
POSTGRES_PASSWORD when it initialises an empty data directory, so a station with
an existing ./timescale_data keeps FooBarHelloWorld until it is rotated with
ALTER ROLE. New installs from this image get the installer's value.
